  "mechanisms": [
    "The attentional narrowing mechanism suggests that noises increase arousal, and at a relatively low arousal level, proper attentional narrowing helps to exclude irrelevant cues, facilitating performance.",
    "Low-arousal speech noises improved memory performance by facilitating attentional narrowing, which helps to exclude irrelevant cues.",
    "P2 and P3 amplitudes reflect cognitive resource allocation and attentional processing. Larger amplitudes in low arousal suggest more resources allocated to the task."
  ],
  "moderators": [
    "Memory load (1-back vs. 3-back)",
    "Arousal level of speech noise (low, high, silent)"
  ],
  "implementation_implications": [
    "Low-arousal speech noises could potentially be used to improve memory performance in certain memory-related tasks.",
    "The findings improve understanding of background noise effects on human performance and lay the ground for investigation of patients with attention deficits."
  ],
  "domains": [
    "A5_Acoustic",
    "A9_Task_Cognition"
  ],
